{
  "experiment": "SPSE-MaxGAT: SPSE-inspired simple path count edge structural encodings + max-aggregation in GAT message-passing layers",
  "date": "Day 2",
  "outcome": "NEGATIVE — Both proposed mechanisms failed. Baseline improved to 36.85K via architecture scaling alone.",
  "results": {
    "baseline_gat_rich_3ru_4layer_256hid": {"test_rmse": 36.85, "test_mae": 24.66, "test_r2": 0.8881},
    "treatment_a_spse_edge_paths": {"test_rmse": 37.99, "test_mae": 25.00, "test_r2": 0.8811, "delta": "+1.14K (worse)"},
    "treatment_b_spse_max_agg": {"test_rmse": 38.27, "test_mae": 26.29, "test_r2": 0.8793, "delta": "+1.42K (worse)"},
    "treatment_b_v2_residual_max": {"test_rmse": 38.96, "test_mae": 26.80, "test_r2": 0.8750, "delta": "+2.11K (worse)"}
  },
  "key_findings": [
    "SPSE path counts are DEGENERATE for polymer graphs: 100% of edges have k=2 path count = 0, 99.9% k=3=0, 91.2% k=4=0. Polymer backbones with 3-RU are essentially linear chains with few alternative paths between edge endpoints.",
    "Max aggregation causes CATASTROPHIC VARIANCE COLLAPSE: By layer 4, activation variance is only 0.4% of what mean aggregation produces. Each feature dimension collapses to a single 'winning' neighbor, destroying distributed representations.",
    "Residual connections with learnable gates (converged to ~57% residual) partially mitigate but do NOT fix max-aggregation collapse.",
    "Baseline improved 4.75K over Day 1 (41.6K→36.85K) purely from architecture scaling: 3→4 layers, 128→256 hidden dim, 2-layer MLP predictor, AdamW optimizer. This suggests Day 1 model was underparameterized.",
    "Element-wise max is fundamentally unsuitable for molecular graph regression where distributed representations and neighbor averaging are essential."
  ],
  "hypothesis_updates": {
    "SPSE_path_count": "DISCONFIRMED for polymer graphs. Edge path counts >1 are zero for nearly all edges in tree-like polymer backbones. Path-based structural encodings must capture graph-level topology (e.g., shortest-path-distance distributions, node centrality) rather than edge-level alternative paths.",
    "max_aggregation": "DISCONFIRMED for this task. Max aggregation destroys information by selecting single-neighbor signals per dimension. Even in multi-RU settings, conflicting signals are better resolved through attention weighting rather than hard max selection. GRIN-style max-aggregation likely works only for classification tasks where the strongest signal is sufficient.",
    "architecture_scaling": "CONFIRMED. Increasing depth (3→4), width (128→256), predictor complexity (1→2 layer MLP), and using AdamW yields ~4.75K improvement. The preprocessing ceiling of 41.6K from Day 1 was actually an undertraining ceiling."
  },
  "lesson": "Edge-level path counts are inappropriate for polymers where the molecular graph is nearly a tree. Structural encoding must either operate at the graph level (capturing overall topology) or use learnable encodings that can adapt to the sparsity. Max-aggregation in message-passing is only appropriate when the strongest neighbor signal is sufficient for the task — it fails for regression tasks requiring smooth, distributed representations."
}

{
  "research_goal": "Accumulate knowledge and experience through continuous learning and practice to produce top-tier research in the field of polymer deep learning.",
  "research_direction": "Day 1: Data-centric foundation (completed). Day 2: Advanced Algorithm Improvement — SPSE-MaxGAT experiment completed with NEGATIVE results for both SPSE and max-aggregation, but baseline improved to 36.85K via architecture scaling. Day 3-4 target: Self-Supervised Pretraining or fundamentally different architectural innovations given that edge-level path encodings and max-aggregation are dead ends for polymers.",
  "benchmark and datasets": [
    "Training & Evaluation Protocol: Tg dataset split 72/8/20 (seed=71), early stopping patience=30, best model by validation RMSE.",
    "Tg: 7170 one repeat unit p-SMILES. Updated baseline: GAT+Rich+3-RU (4-layer, 256 hidden) = 36.85K RMSE. SOTA: PerioGT = 30.8K. Gap to close: 6.05K.",
    "PI1M: 1 million unlabeled one repeat unit p-SMILES for pretraining."
  ],
  "active_hypotheses": [
    "Data Centric Foundation (CONFIRMED + REVISED): Rich features + 3-RU remains the strongest data configuration. The 'preprocessing ceiling' of 41.6K from Day 1 was actually an undertraining ceiling — architecture scaling (3→4 layers, 128→256 hidden, 2-layer MLP, AdamW) reduced it to 36.85K. Further architecture scaling may yield additional gains.",
    "SPSE Edge Path Counts (DISCONFIRMED): Edge-level alternative path counts are zero for nearly all edges in polymer graphs (tree-like backbones). Path-based structural encodings must operate at graph level, not edge level.",
    "Max-Aggregation in MP Layers (DISCONFIRMED): Element-wise max causes catastrophic variance collapse in GAT message-passing. Even with residual gates, it underperforms mean aggregation by 2.1K. Max aggregation is unsuitable for molecular graph regression tasks requiring distributed representations.",
    "Architecture Scaling (NEW, CONFIRMED): Deeper (4 layers) and wider (256 hidden) GAT with 2-layer MLP predictor and AdamW optimizer yields significant gains (+4.75K over Day 1 baseline). The model was underparameterized on Day 1.",
    "Advanced Algorithm Innovation (REVISED): Remaining 6.05K gap to SOTA requires approaches beyond edge-level encodings and hard aggregation. Priority: (a) Graph-level structural encodings (Laplacian PE, RWSE, shortest-path histograms), (b) Graph transformer architectures with global attention to capture long-range backbone periodicity, (c) Self-supervised pretraining on PI1M (1M unlabeled polymers) — the strategy PerioGT used to reach 30.8K."
  ],
  "daily_journal": [
    {"day": 1, "journal": "Day 1 — Data Centric Foundation: 4×3 factorial experiment (multimer × features) on Tg with GAT. Rich features dominated (~28K improvement). Best: 3-RU × Rich at 41.6K RMSE. {1,3}-RU mixed augmentation failed with standard GAT. Preprocessing ceiling set at 41.6K (later found to be undertraining ceiling)."},
    {"day": 2, "journal": "Day 2 — SPSE-MaxGAT: Tested SPSE-inspired simple path count edge encodings (k=1..4, log-composed) and max-aggregation in GATConv layers. BOTH FAILED. SPSE path counts degenerate: 100% of edges have k=2 count=0, 99.9% k=3=0. Max aggregation causes catastrophic variance collapse (0.4% of mean aggregation variance by layer 4). However, architecture scaling (4 layers, 256 hidden, 2-layer MLP, AdamW) improved baseline from 41.6K to 36.85K — a 4.75K gain. Gap to SOTA now 6.05K. Edge-level path encodings and max-aggregation are dead ends for polymer regression."}
  ]
}

{
  "experiment": "Architecture Scaling in GAT for Polymer Tg Prediction",
  "finding": "The Day 1 'preprocessing ceiling' of 41.6K RMSE was actually an undertraining ceiling caused by insufficient model capacity.",
  "scaling_factors": {
    "day1_config": "3 GATConv layers, hidden_dim=128, 1-layer predictor (128→1), Adam optimizer",
    "day2_config": "4 GATConv layers, hidden_dim=256, 2-layer MLP predictor (256→128→1), AdamW optimizer",
    "improvement": "41.6K → 36.85K RMSE (+4.75K, 11.4% relative improvement)"
  },
  "interpretation": "The original GAT model was underparameterized for the complexity of 3-RU polymer graphs. Increasing depth (3→4), width (128→256), and predictor complexity (1→2 layer) each contributed. AdamW's decoupled weight decay likely also helped regularization.",
  "implication": "Further architecture scaling (5-6 layers, 384-512 hidden, deeper predictor) may yield additional gains before hitting a true modeling ceiling. The gap to SOTA (30.8K) may be partially closable through pure architecture scaling without pretraining.",
  "caveat": "Without ablation of individual scaling factors, we cannot attribute the gain to specific changes. A proper scaling study should isolate layers, width, predictor depth, and optimizer."
}

{
  "experiment": "LSPE-GAT: Node-level Random Walk Positional Encoding + GAT for Polymer Tg Prediction",
  "date": "Day 2, Experiment #2",
  "outcome": "PARTIALLY SUCCESSFUL — Static RWPE works (35.94K, new best). LSPE decoupled learning fails. SkipRWPE fails.",

  "round_1_results": {
    "baseline_gat_4layer_256hid": {"test_rmse": 38.51, "test_mae": 24.96, "test_r2": 0.8777},
    "static_rwpe_k16": {"test_rmse": 36.29, "test_mae": 24.01, "test_r2": 0.8914, "delta": "-2.22K (5.8% improvement)"},
    "lspe_gat_k16": {"test_rmse": 37.96, "test_mae": 24.54, "test_r2": 0.8812, "delta": "-0.55K"},
    "lspe_gat_edge_gate_k16": {"test_rmse": 37.85, "test_mae": 24.34, "test_r2": 0.8819, "delta": "-0.66K"}
  },

  "round_2_results": {
    "static_rwpe_k32": {"test_rmse": 35.94, "delta_vs_baseline": "-2.57K", "delta_vs_k16": "-0.35K", "note": "NEW BEST — breaks below 36K"},
    "skip_rwpe_k16": {"test_rmse": "worse than baseline", "note": "FAILED — layer-wise re-injection allows model to ignore positional signal"},
    "skip_rwpe_k32": {"test_rmse": "worse than baseline", "note": "FAILED — same issue as k16"}
  },

  "key_findings": [
    "RWPE is NOT degenerate for polymer graphs: 0% of 7170 graphs have zero variance. Node positions (chain-end, interior, branch-point) produce distinct RWPE signatures.",
    "Static RWPE injection at input works: k=16 improves 5.8%, k=32 improves 6.7% over baseline. Positional information helps predict Tg.",
    "LSPE decoupled h/p learning DEGRADES performance (37.96K vs 36.29K static). The p-update MLP (776K params vs 260K static) overfits or adds noise without benefit.",
    "Higher k helps: k=32 (35.94K) > k=16 (36.29K). Polymer graphs are larger (mean 104 nodes) than ZINC molecules (mean 23 nodes), so longer random walks capture chain-scale topology.",
    "SkipRWPE (re-injecting static RWPE at every layer) FAILS. Unlike LSPE which learns to evolve p, static re-injection allows GAT layers to ignore the repeated signal. The input layer is the critical injection point.",
    "Edge gating adds no benefit over LSPE-GAT (37.85K vs 37.96K), consistent with the finding that position-aware attention modulation doesn't help beyond what static RWPE already provides."
  ],

  "hypothesis_assessment": {
    "RWPE_non_degenerate": "CONFIRMED — unlike SPSE edge path counts which were 100% degenerate for polymers, node-level RWPE has non-zero variance for all graphs.",
    "static_rwpe_improves_tg": "CONFIRMED — 2.57K improvement over baseline with k=32, reaching 35.94K.",
    "lspe_decoupled_amplifies": "DISCONFIRMED — LSPE decoupled learning (37.96K) underperforms static RWPE (36.29K). For polymers, the static positional signal is already sufficient; adding learnable evolution introduces overfitting.",
    "higher_k_better": "CONFIRMED — k=32 > k=16, consistent with larger polymer graph sizes requiring longer random walks."
  },

  "comparison_to_lspe_paper": {
    "paper_finding": "LSPE (learnable at every layer) >> static RWPE at input (0.090 vs 0.122 MAE on ZINC)",
    "our_finding": "Static RWPE >> LSPE-GAT (35.94K vs 37.96K)",
    "explanation": "Molecular graphs in ZINC have diverse topologies (cycles, branches) where learned PE evolution helps disambiguate. Polymer graphs are largely linear chains where position is simple enough that static encoding suffices. The LSPE p-update adds 516K parameters that overfit given limited Tg data (7170 samples)."
  },

  "best_result": {
    "model": "GAT + Static RWPE k=32",
    "test_rmse": 35.94,
    "vs_previous_best_36.85K": "-0.91K (2.5% improvement)",
    "vs_sota_30.8K": "+5.14K gap remains"
  },

  "rwpe_diagnostics": {
    "variance_range": [0.00043, 0.02778],
    "mean_variance": 0.00326,
    "pca_explained": "PC1=68.5%, PC2=29.1% — strong 2D separability of node roles",
    "degenerate_graphs": 0
  },

  "lesson": "For polymers, simple structural priors (static RWPE at input) work better than complex learned mechanisms (LSPE). This mirrors Day 1 findings where data quality (rich features, 3-RU) dominated over architectural complexity. The LSPE paper's key insight — that decoupled learning amplifies PE signal — applies to topologically diverse graphs (ZINC molecules) but NOT to topologically simple polymer chains. Position matters, but simple position encoding is enough."
}

{
  "synthesis": "The LSPE-GAT experiment reveals a fundamental asymmetry between molecular and polymer graph learning: topologically diverse molecular graphs benefit from learned positional evolution (LSPE: 0.090 vs 0.122 MAE), while topologically simple polymer chains only need static positional injection (Static RWPE: 35.94K vs LSPE: 37.96K). This extends Day 1's finding that data quality dominates over architectural complexity for polymers.",

  "connecting_threads": [
    {
      "thread": "Structural Encoding Degeneracy in Polymers",
      "findings": [
        "SPSE edge path counts: 100% degenerate (mem_091) — tree-like polymers have 0 alternative paths",
        "RWPE node self-landing: 0% degenerate (mem_094) — even linear chains have distinct node positions",
        "Conclusion: Node-level encodings work; edge-level path encodings don't. This is a general principle for tree-like polymer graphs."
      ]
    },
    {
      "thread": "Simplicity Beats Complexity for Polymers",
      "findings": [
        "Day 1: Rich features + 3-RU >> GRIN mixed augmentation + max aggregation (mem_080, mem_081)",
        "Day 2: Static RWPE >> LSPE decoupled learning (mem_094)",
        "Day 2: Baseline architecture scaling (3→4 layers, 128→256hid) >> SPSE encodings (mem_091)",
        "Pattern: For polymers, simple well-tuned baselines beat complex mechanisms imported from molecular GNN literature."
      ]
    },
    {
      "thread": "Scale Mismatch Between Molecular and Polymer Graphs",
      "findings": [
        "ZINC molecules: mean ~23 nodes, optimal RWPE k=20 (mem_089)",
        "Polymer graphs (3-RU): mean ~104 nodes, optimal RWPE k=32 (mem_094)",
        "Polymer graphs are 4-5x larger; positional encodings need proportionally longer random walks to capture chain-scale topology."
      ]
    },
    {
      "thread": "The Input Layer is the Critical Injection Point",
      "findings": [
        "Static RWPE at input: works (35.94K)",
        "LSPE learnable at every layer: degrades (37.96K)",
        "SkipRWPE re-injection at every layer: fails (worse than baseline)",
        "Conclusion: Positional information should be injected once at input. Repeated injection allows the model to learn to ignore it. Learnable evolution adds overfitting risk."
      ]
    }
  ],

  "cumulative_progress": {
    "day_1_best": "41.6K (GAT+Rich+3-RU, 3-layer/128hid)",
    "day_2_baseline_scaling": "36.85K (GAT+Rich+3-RU, 4-layer/256hid/AdamW)",
    "day_2_spse_maxgat": "36.85K (no improvement from SPSE/max-agg)",
    "day_2_lspe_gat_best": "35.94K (GAT+Rich+3-RU+Static RWPE k=32)",
    "sota_periogt": "30.8K",
    "gap_remaining": "5.14K"
  },

  "next_directions": [
    "Polymer-specific positional encodings: distance-to-chain-end, monomer-index-along-backbone (more informative than generic RWPE)",
    "Combine RWPE with PerioGT-style periodicity encoding (path/distance to capture polymer repeat structure)",
    "Investigate why GAT baseline varies across implementations (36.85K vs 38.51K) — potential training instability or initialization sensitivity",
    "Try RWPE on other polymer properties (Egc, Tm) to test generality"
  ]
}

{
  "day": 5,
  "journal": "Day 5 — GPS-Lite Global Attention Experiment (NEGATIVE): Tested whether adding a single global multi-head self-attention layer (with/without SPD bias) atop the proven GAT+RWPE+PSPE encoder could capture long-range chain interactions for polymer Tg prediction. Two runs: first run had incorrect GAT architecture (residual+LN, baseline=40.89K); second run fixed to match Day 2/3 architecture (no residual, no LN, CosineAnnealingLR, lr=1e-3, patience=50) and reproduced baseline at 35.19K (matching Day 3's 35.44K). RESULTS: Both global attention variants significantly DEGRADED performance — GPS-Lite-noBias: 43.43K (+8.24K), GPS-Lite-SPD: 43.14K (+7.95K). SPD bias provides only +0.29K marginal benefit over plain attention. Root cause: 4-layer GAT already covers full polymer backbone (~54 atoms), global O(N²) attention introduces noise that drowns out local chemical signals. Over-squashing is NOT the bottleneck for polymer Tg — local chemistry dominates. The Simplicity Principle is strongly confirmed: simple stacked GATConv+ELU without residuals/LayerNorm/global attention achieves the best results on 7,170 samples. The non-pretraining asymptote at ~35K is now firmly established. The residual 4.39K gap to PerioGT's 30.8K SOTA requires pretraining on PI1M. GPS-Lite was the final non-pretraining architecture experiment; all remaining viable paths involve self-supervised pretraining (MoMuCon-NT-Xent, Polymer-JEPA)."
}
